WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.450
An island arc forms where one oceanic plate subducts beneath another.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.930
The descending plate bends into a trench.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:10.350
As it heats, metamorphic reactions release water from minerals.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.450
That water promotes melting in the mantle above the slab.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:17.630
Magma rises through the overriding plate, so the volcanic belt lies

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.770
above the mantle wedge rather than directly in the trench.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:24.670
Submarine eruptions first build volcanic mountains under water.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.810
Continued magmatism can raise islands, thicken their crust and connect them

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:33.690
into longer landmasses. Interaction between mantle-derived magma and the growing crust

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.570
can produce intermediate compositions and dangerous composite volcanoes.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:42.670
Java and the Alaska Peninsula illustrate substantial land built along a

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:46.830
volcanic arc. The trench and volcanic belt are related but distinct

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:47.310
features.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:51.270
A dense slab can sink and roll back, shifting the trench

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:53.030
away from the arc's former position.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:56.570
The volcanic belt follows the changing slab geometry.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.510
Extension within or behind that migrating arc can split its crust

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.570
and create a back-arc spreading center.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:06.250
Convergence at the trench and divergence behind the arc can operate

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:06.950
at the same time.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:12.050
The textbook's globe comparison explains why the trench and arc can

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:15.430
curve. A plate bends into a spherical Earth and must fit

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.470
a smaller available width at depth;

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:19.090
the descending slab can buckle.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:23.030
A flat cross section is useful for understanding subduction, but it

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.270
cannot display the complete map-view geometry.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.490
Compare that section with the curved trench and island belt in

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:29.290
the original figure.

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:36.010
Major examples include the Aleutians, Japan and the Marianas, Tonga, Sumatra

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:39.990
and Java, the Lesser Antilles, the Aegean and Scotia arcs.

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.770
Some systems interact with continental crust, so the details vary.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:49.510
For any example, identify the incoming plate, trench, overriding plate, volcanoes

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:53.190
and any back-arc basin. An island chain over a hot spot

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.070
does not automatically have this trench-and-slab relationship.
